  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several large-scale pathways by which tropical oceans and the upper atmosphere appear to influence rainfall over Africa and nearby regions. Across all panels, broad gray arrows depict an overturning tropical circulation linking the Pacific, Indian, Atlantic, and African sectors, while colored shading marks warm or cool sea-surface anomalies and blue downward arrows indicate subsiding air over Africa.\n\nThe top panel highlights a tropospheric temperature effect: warm upper-level anomalies spread eastward as Kelvin waves and are associated with sinking motion over northern tropical Africa. The lower panels compare seasonal oceanic influences. In boreal autumn, a positive Indian Ocean Dipole is shown with contrasting Indian Ocean temperature patterns and rising convection there, connected to changes over the tropical Pacific and descending branches farther west. In boreal winter, Indian Ocean basin warming appears to reorganize the circulation again, with convection over the western Indian Ocean and altered low-level flow toward Africa. In boreal summer, the Atlantic becomes the main driver: an “Atlantic Niña” cool anomaly in the equatorial Atlantic is linked to subsidence over Africa and circulation shifts spanning the Pacific and Indian Oceans.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes season-dependent ocean–atmosphere teleconnections that modulate African climate through convection and subsidence.",
